<h3>The original&rsquo;s interactivity — what &ldquo;moves&rdquo;</h3>
<p>ai-2040.com is not a plain article but an <strong>interactive document</strong>. The confirmed elements are:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Step-by-step, branching narrative</strong> — following the scenario in stages and unfolding the other options at each fork, a <code>choices</code> structure (<a href="https://ai-2040.com/?choices=plan-a-root" target="_blank" rel="noopener">plan-a-root</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Modified AI Futures Model explorer</strong> — an interactive for adjusting, yourself, the model that produces Plan A&rsquo;s capability trajectory.</li>
    <li><strong>Economic growth explorer</strong> — modeling the economy of the AI scenario with factors such as capital, robots, prices, land and energy (<a href="https://ai-2040.com/supplements" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Supplements</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Audio, mobile and desktop editions</strong> — the experience is richest on desktop.</li>
</ul>
<p class="scn-sub"><strong>Applied to this wiki too?</strong> — the <strong>clickable timeline</strong> above is the answer. The step-by-step interaction has been <strong>reproduced self-containedly, without external libraries</strong>, in this wiki&rsquo;s design language (theme tokens, lightweight JS). The full economic model simulator, by contrast, is too large in scope, so it is left as a static summary plus a link to the original.</p>

<div class="note warn">
    <span class="nh">The boundary between fact and estimate</span>
    <strong>Confirmed facts</strong>: the document&rsquo;s existence, its authors and release date, its character as &ldquo;a recommendation, not a prediction&rdquo;, the core substance of delay to 2040, total transparency and mutually assured compute destruction, and the existence of the reactions. <strong>Estimated and contested territory</strong>: the <strong>feasibility</strong> of this plan (it presumes US–China trust and state capacity), the assumption that superintelligence arrives in 2030, and the realism of relocating data centers — the authors themselves state that it is &ldquo;a device for pressing and testing policy, not a prediction.&rdquo;
</div>

